# Documentation, Errors, Logging & Testing

## Comments & documentation (required)
- Every public function/method/class gets a doc comment: purpose, parameters,
  return value, and errors/exceptions it can raise.
- Inline comments explain WHY (intent, trade-offs, edge cases), not WHAT.
- Mark security-sensitive logic, e.g. `SECURITY: validates resource owner`.
- Use `TODO:` / `FIXME:` with a short reason — never a context-free TODO.
- Keep comments truthful and updated when the code changes.

## Error handling
- Never swallow errors silently (no empty catch blocks).
- Fail fast on programmer errors; handle and recover from operational errors.
- Use a consistent error/response shape across the codebase.
- Clean up resources in finally/defer/using blocks.
- Validate preconditions at the top of functions; return early on failure.

## Logging & observability
- Use structured logging (key/value), not bare prints.
- Log auth success/failure, access denials, and admin actions.
- NEVER log secrets or sensitive PII — redact first.
- Include trace context (request id, user id) for debugging.

## Testing
- New logic ships with tests. Auth, payments, and data mutations MUST be tested.
- Cover happy path, boundaries, and at least one failure/abuse case.
- Tests must be deterministic and independent (no shared mutable state; no real
  network/DB unless integration-tagged).
- Name tests by behaviour: `should_<expected>_when_<condition>`.
